this morning on the dish a fourth of  july barbecue courtesy of oakland  california's self-taught pit master matt  horn the 34 year old has gained  legendary status in the east bay named  one of food and wine's best new chefs  last year while his restaurant horn  barbecue earned a michelin bib gourmand  award and was a james beard finalist for  best new restaurant horn's journey from  novice to award-winning chef is  chronicled in his new cookbook named for  his restaurant and meant to serve as  both barbecue guide and inspiration to  at home cooks who've dreamed of more  it's the barbecue that draws customers  in droves we just hit our brakes  furiously when we smelled the goodness  coming out of this place horn barbecue  in oakland this one right here since  opening about two years ago it's been  known for both its snaking lines  gotcha and mouth-watering meats and  sides i'm going pulled pork i might get  the pit beans maybe some mac dubbed west  coast style barbecue its roots are in  texas and the south the best barbecue  i've had outside of texas  well this is like our primary  cut of meat this is what everybody lines  up for so this is the brisket this is  the brisket  for pit master matt horn barbecue isn't  just the finished product it's a journey  the smoke will come and it'll go right  over it from prepping and seasoning the  meat  to stoking the fire that brings it to  light  barbecue is more than just throwing logs  in there and that sort of thing it's  very careful fire management you know  you have to maintain that temperature  and how long does it smoke for so we  usually smoke our briskets for about 14  to 16 hours it's definitely a commitment  you know it's a labor of love  and appreciation horn learned along the  way i mean in the beginning i'm just  like okay i want to hurry up and finish  the cook and you find yourself  popping the lid and looking you know  what i did was i started to realize that  throughout the cooking journey you want  to trust the smoker to do what it was  built to do be one with the fire  be in the moment be present and that's  what i've learned to do  this is the finished product right here  patience is now a virtue for horn but  not for me matt i'm drooling can i  please taste absolutely  there you go enjoy  it melts in your mouth  oh my god  i'm gonna be very unladylike i have to  taste this  jalapeno cheddar right yep jalapeno  cheddar i got a full spread to sample in  a backyard barbecue starting with  homemade jalapeno cheddar sausage links  i used to always see my grandfather  eating the hot links he would take it  right off the grill and he would grab a  piece of white bread and just do it he  would just do it that way that nod to  family in everything from the smoked  chicken pulled pork and ribs to the pit  beans collard greens and mac and cheese  grandma he says gets credit for the  dessert you gotta have banana pudding oh  this is delicious  like the barbecue he serves horn's  journey in it can be traced to childhood  when his grandfather was the pit master  whether it was a wedding a funeral a  birthday or just a family gathering you  know you'd always smell the smoke the  fire would be lit and there would always  be a barbecue in his early 20s cooking  became horn's escape from his day job he  soon realized it was even more not only  did it give me purpose but i felt  fulfilled with what i was doing in my  life finally for the first time in my  life once i decided that i want to start  cooking you know it was a clear choice  that i wanted to do barbecue because  that was the thing that brought us  together as a family to hone the craft  of barbecue horn turned to where it all  began i would drive down from los  angeles every weekend to my  grandmother's backyard and i take a  notepad and i grab a few cuts of meat  and i will fire the pit up and i would  take extremely extensive notes your  grandfather's pit oh yeah and that was  the only place where i can really  barbecue after a few months in the  backyard with family taste testers horn  headed to a farmer's market to sell his  barbecue i had about a little under a  couple hundred bucks  and i bought as much meat as i can with  that and i just literally believed in  like hey if i don't cut corners and i  contribute love and commit myself to  this someone will connect with it and we  went through the farmer's market for a  year  and we struggle i even begin to question  am i doing the right thing what changed  your mind i took a few days off and i  didn't cook at all and i found myself  laying in bed  thinking about the smoke the fire  all these beautiful elements that make  barbecue what it is and i'm just like  you know what i have to continue to push  forward with this  that feeling was validated when horn  launched his very first pop-up barbecue  i sat in my truck and i was really  emotional why because i was grateful  that they even came to even to enjoy the  food and then the next papa we had is 50  people there  and then the next one you know over 100  but little did i know that it would turn  into what it turned into it was one of  the largest pop-ups  in the country and  hundreds of people waiting hours i mean  i mean i can't even yeah and they would  set up their lawn chairs they would set  up tents people do that for sporting  events you've gotten that sword  right i know and it was a beautiful  thing and i always made it an effort to  go out and shake everyone's hand and  thank everybody for coming no matter how  long it took  when his restaurant became a reality in  the same area where his pop-ups became  legendary horn made sure to include  reminders of his journey  including his smoker lucille there from  the start her name a tribute to the  iconic bb king song  lucille  i would find myself in all these  different places  farms  at breweries and parking lots alleys  whatever  sitting there by the fire i'd have  lucille by b.b king playing every single  time there was always something about  that song that always made me  i don't know it always connected me back  to the purpose of why i do what i do  with barbecue  he says it's his way of giving love  something he's getting back in return  this thought that i had  to commit myself to cooking  it's brought me to where i am here now  you know and i wish both of my  grandfathers was here to just see like  hey you know what that thing that  brought our family together the thing  that we've all connected to  i've taken that and i'm sharing it with  my community and  we're getting the recognition for it and  we're grateful  and i have had some scent to share with  you couple things i want to mention that  farmer's market when he was working  there never made more than  250 dollars a day but persevered as he  talked about it's now actually horn  hospitality group they have cowbird also  in oakland which is a chicken place  i'm telling you you got a taste it's it  is absolutely appealing and such an  inspiring story of somebody seeing a  vision having a vision and really  sticking with it because it was his love  and his passion and it fulfilled him and  it tastes really really good right it's  delicious um  obviously we have the brisket i should  mention we have the jalapeno sausage  jalapeno cheddar sausage the ribs the  mac and cheese collard greens and cheers  cheers to that right
 

